#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175992 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c597f9f4-f054-55e9-90de-8b8fc3d1bd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x - 7}{x - 1}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173705} {:answer/id #uuid "058306c3-78b2-5d5b-82f9-3445563225af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{2}}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173706} {:answer/id #uuid "180c98b4-67a3-5980-8b6a-3f1d9d35e91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x - 7}{x - 1}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173707} {:answer/id #uuid "338d71c3-4da2-5e8c-917c-bcbfd95cec5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x - 5}{x - 1}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173708} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09eaa7-b7e1-55ed-a5ec-22a9c822c39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173709}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2122b91f-e6b1-53a7-bc39-72f4271ba65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173704}] :question/id #uuid "49aa78c4-2d01-5b28-b29f-36722ae18536" :question/math-academy-id "q-109424" :question/problem "![](images/58/5806f57e41eeb5548b0c041533adf3952dd7f35236121f9056c57f03c2854ddc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y = \\sqrt{1 - x}$ that lies in the first quadrant between $x = 0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd93bd0-5caf-5cdf-9fec-3bc7b09fd51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250663} {:answer/id #uuid "1f021ce1-6e5b-56e4-ba92-6b150b40127b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250816} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d57bd8-e7b5-52b2-aa74-d3c445be8ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251947} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b8f5ab-73ab-512f-b3cc-a7df929a3e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252171} {:answer/id #uuid "ee065c7b-b94e-5f3b-a32b-5b87650db484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1d57bd8-e7b5-52b2-aa74-d3c445be8ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251947} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6690fcb-b915-5e44-8c44-d66de9e23524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69d6c7c8-a17d-5595-94e0-1820b1ec12de" :question/math-academy-id "q-119290" :question/problem "A population of $50\\,000\\,000$ people is being vaccinated against a disease. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ vaccinated is jointly proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ measured in months, and the number of unvaccinated people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.3,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of vaccinated people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ and the number of unvaccinated people, the number of vaccinated people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have been vaccinated is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381464a8-f1f7-542d-a442-763dabbf0722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330448} {:answer/id #uuid "52ccb450-b1ee-5c92-99d3-724f5548b247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330702} {:answer/id #uuid "831193f3-07ba-55f2-8e65-22822c9ce5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331112} {:answer/id #uuid "9f76c378-2dc9-5123-ac6f-b8fd6a7ce8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331385} {:answer/id #uuid "efb7e2c1-6247-5e8f-8d91-f3e2b9a5e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb7e2c1-6247-5e8f-8d91-f3e2b9a5e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332054} :answer-field/id #uuid "51abc2de-d0da-5fce-aa24-dac010401f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e19633c-fd26-592a-9173-f3506939c283" :question/math-academy-id "q-50233" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2D \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"}]}
